# The hand-written key for intent/rules on CardDemo (phase 5.2, research §29): what a person reading each
# program says, written before any rule; corrected after the first run for what its making got wrong, each
# correction in J-195 (CBSTM03A, absent when its survey read CUSTREC.cpy at 8 columns; three items with one
# condition-name, counted as two). A line names a fact that holds: `enum PROGRAM ITEM`, `money PROGRAM
# ITEM`, `function PROGRAM FIRST [THRU LAST]`. Within each scope, a candidate with no line is a fact that does
# not hold. Written by Opus, 2026-10-17, from each declaration and paragraph read; for Ghost's review.
#
# scope enum: every item with two or more condition-names, in every CardDemo program. An enum holds one of
#   several named values; a yes-or-no (Y/N, 0/1, valid/invalid, on/off, a LOW-VALUE and one other) is a flag,
#   not an enum; a free-text message field with named texts, and a number with named values or ranges (a day,
#   a month, a century), are neither, nor is a code checked against named lists of valid values (an area
#   code). A three-state edit result (valid, not ok, blank) is an enum.
# scope flag: every item with one or two condition-names, in every CardDemo program (added 2026-10-18 for
#   R-065's flag rule, before it was written). A flag is a yes-or-no: two condition-names that split its
#   values (Y/N, 0/1, valid/invalid, on/off, a LOW-VALUE and one other), or one that names the yes (found,
#   in error, invalid) of a switch or a group of switches. A status code, a number, a list of valid codes,
#   a message and a storage marker are not, whatever their condition-names.
# scope money: every numeric item of scale 2 (V99), in every CardDemo program. Money but DIS-INT-RATE, an
#   interest rate in percent. Numeric-edited report fields are out of scope.
# scope function: every PERFORM range in the batch programs (CB*, CSUTLDTC, COBSWAIT). A range is a function
#   when only a PERFORM enters it, it leaves only at its end (a GO TO to its own exit stays inside), no ALTER
#   reaches it, it does not PERFORM itself, and no other range overlaps it; ending the run (GOBACK, a CALL to
#   CEE3ABD) is not leaving it. Every batch range read is one. The online programs are out of scope: their
#   main lines end in EXEC CICS RETURN, which the IR cannot read before the CICS facade (7.2).
